import <wdoc.wcl>

fact math {
  title = "math"
  related = []
  audience = :both
  body {
    p <<DOC
      A `math` block typesets a LaTeX equation to inline SVG via the pure-Rust RaTeX toolchain.
      Glyph outlines embed in the SVG, so equations render the same whether the page is opened
      locally or served — no webfont dependency.
    DOC
    demo {
      math "E = mc^2"
      math "\\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\n"
    }
    type_table {
      type = Math
    }
    h2 "Block equations"
    p <<DOC
      `math "…" {}` (or `math <<TEX … TEX`) renders a centred display equation. Use a raw heredoc
      for backslash-heavy LaTeX.
    DOC
    h2 "Inline math"
    p <<DOC
      Inside any `p` body or `span`, two inline patterns produce math: `$…$` is text-style (flows
      with the prose) and `$$…$$` is display-style (slightly larger, still inline). The `$…$`
      pattern requires a non-space at each delimiter, so currency stays untouched. See
      [formatting](concept_formatting).
    DOC
    demo {
      p <<'DOC'
        The quadratic root $x = \frac{-b \pm \sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}$ flows inline with the prose,
        while a display-style sum $$\sum_{i=1}^{n} i = \frac{n(n+1)}{2}$$ sits slightly larger but
        still in the line.
      DOC
    }
    table {
      header = ["Pattern", "Style"]
      rows = [["$…$", "Text-style inline math — flows with the surrounding prose."], ["$$…$$", "Display-style inline math — slightly larger, still inline."]]
    }
  }
}
